the violence in Hong Kong entered a  dangerous new phase as pro-democracy  demonstrators entered their twelfth week  protesters and police clashed repeatedly  over the weekend rainy Inocencio was in  Hong Kong where he's been reporting on  these protests since they started good  morning good morning last night we saw  the most aggressive violence in weeks  right here protesters threw bricks and  Molotov cocktails trying to provoke the  police and they did beaten and chased by  a mob of protesters police fired the  first shot of these protests a warning  into the air saying later they felt  their lives were in danger hand guns  drawn they push back the rushing crowd  and kicked an older man on his knees who  begged them not to shoot also new to the  street two water cannons deployed to the  residential district of chinlock where  protesters and police clashed in a  closed standoff for hours now that the  water cannons have hit the streets  protesters have fled into the alleys all  around us after nightfall groups of  masked protesters once again used the  subways to fan out quickly attacking and  retreating before police could arrive  they smashed toll booths at across  Harvard tunnel destroyed shops thought  to be owned by China League gangs and  blocked major roads authorities seemed  unable or unwilling to stop the violence  the latest payoffs in this one stable  Asian financial hub that shows no signs  of stopping police say they arrested  three dozen people last night the  youngest just 12 years old pro-beijing  legislators have said the violence is  now life-threatening many fear people  could die that's adding to fears that  Beijing could deploy troops to pacify  this already embattled city for CBS this  morning  ramie Inocencio Hong Kong yes we're  getting worse thank you Raymond
 

